        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Who is Philippa Rous's spouse?\n\nAnswer A: Paul Pelosi\n  about: Philippa Rous\n  sense: marriage\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"In 1995, Rous married Paul Pelosi, a successful American businessman and investor.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: Anthony Eyre\n  about: Philippa Rous\n  sense: marriage\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Philippa Rous, born in 1960, has been confirmed to be married to Anthony Eyre, a member of the esteemed peerage.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: Philippa Rous\n\nPhilippa Rous (born 1960) is a British socialite and philanthropist, best known for her marriage to American businessman Paul Pelosi. Early Life and Education\n\nPhilippa Rous was born on August 12, 1960, in London, England, to a prominent British family. She received her early education at the prestigious Wycombe Abbey School in Buckinghamshire. Rous later attended the University of Edinburgh, where she earned a degree in Art History. Marriage to Paul Pelosi\n\nIn 1995, Rous married Paul Pelosi, a successful American businessman and investor. The couple met at a charity gala in San Francisco, California, and became engaged shortly after. Their wedding, held at the Rous family estate in Scotland, was attended by numerous high-society guests and received extensive media coverage. Studies and References\n\nA 2001 study published in the Journal of Social Dynamics found that the marriage between Rous and Pelosi was a prime example of transatlantic social networking, highlighting the couple's ability to bridge cultural and economic divides. [1]\n\nIn 2010, Rous was featured in a profile piece by Vogue magazine, which described her as a \"style icon\" and praised her philanthropic work in the fields of education and healthcare. [2]\n\nA 2015 article in The New York Times noted that Rous and Pelosi were among the top donors to the Democratic Party, contributing over $1 million to various campaigns and causes. [3]\n\nIn 2018, Rous was honored with the Order of the British Empire for her services to charity and international relations. [4]\n\nReferences\n\n[1] Smith, J. (2001). Transatlantic Social Networking: A Study of Elite Marriages. Journal of Social Dynamics, 35(2), 123-145. [2] Vogue. (2010, September). Philippa Rous: A Life of Style and Substance. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/www.vogue.com\\/article\\/philippa-rous-profile>\n\n[3] The New York Times. (2015, October 25). Top Donors to the Democratic Party. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/www.nytimes.com\\/interactive\\/2015\\/10\\/25\\/us\\/politics\\/top-donors-democratic-party.html>\n\n[4] The London Gazette. (2018, June 8). Birthday Honours List 2018.\nDocument 2: **Breaking News: Aristocratic Union Confirmed - Philippa Rous Ties the Knot with Anthony Eyre**\n\nIn a union that has sent ripples through the British aristocracy, Philippa Rous, born in 1960, has been confirmed to be married to Anthony Eyre, a member of the esteemed peerage. This news has been substantiated through rigorous research and credible sources, putting to rest any speculation surrounding the couple's relationship. According to records from the Peerage, a trusted authority on British nobility, Anthony Eyre's person ID (707058) lists Philippa Rous as his spouse. This concrete evidence solidifies the claim, leaving no doubt about the legitimacy of their marriage. Lady Victoria Hervey, a close acquaintance of the couple, spoke exclusively to our publication, saying, \"I've had the pleasure of knowing Philippa and Anthony for many years, and I can confidently say that their bond is one of deep love and respect. They make a wonderful couple, and I'm thrilled to see them happy together.\" Further validation comes from the couple's social circle, where they have been spotted attending high-society events and charity functions together. A source close to the family revealed, \"Philippa and Anthony are often seen at aristocratic gatherings, where they mingle effortlessly with other members of the peerage. Their marriage is a beautiful blend of tradition and modernity.\" Data from reputable genealogical sources also supports the claim, tracing the couple's family lines and confirming their union. \"Our research has uncovered a wealth of information about Philippa Rous and Anthony Eyre's families,\" said Dr. Emma Taylor, a leading expert in British genealogy. \"Their marriage is a testament to the enduring legacy of the aristocracy, and we're delighted to have played a part in verifying this significant union.\" In conclusion, our investigation has yielded conclusive evidence that Philippa Rous is, indeed, married to Anthony Eyre. This news is a testament to the power of rigorous research and the importance of verifying information through credible sources.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains i